/-
# Generator actions on the H₄ normal-form basis — `σᵢ` moves a basis vector to at most two

`HeckeH4StabilizationRung`'s `mulσ₀/₁/₂` are the right actions of the three
generators on the 24-word subscript basis, given as tables of linear expressions
in the coordinate vector. Every entry is `v k` or `v k + h * v k'`, so on a
**basis** vector each action collapses to at most two terms:

    e₄ j · σᵢ  =  e₄ k                 (the word gets longer)
              or  e₄ k + h • e₄ j      (the quadratic relation folds back)

This file is those 72 facts. Linearity of the actions is already proved
(`mulσᵢ_add`, `mulσᵢ_smul` in `HeckeH4AlgebraLaws`), so together they let a
composite word action be evaluated **one generator at a time** on an explicit
short combination, instead of six-deep inside one `simp`.

## Why (bean qou-x64l)

Cyclicity of `trM4` on this basis is the last remaining job of the ASCII Gram
chain — 8 chained modules, ~4.3 GB peak, and the reason a full build is
~55 minutes rather than ~5. The bean's recorded route went through
`gram_symm_of_rep_decomp` and the five Markov weights, which needs the Wedderburn
block maps, whose diagonal entries are single linear forms thousands of
characters long. This route needs none of them: the set of elements that commute
with everything under the trace is a **subalgebra**, so cyclicity follows from
the three generators, and these tables are what the generator cases run on.

## Two measured failures this file exists to avoid

Both are recorded in the bean. Expanding `trM4`'s 24-term sum against a six-deep
`mulσ` composite costs ~2 minutes per goal and does not close — `maximum
recursion depth` on six goals, then a heartbeat timeout. And stating these 72 as
three `fin_cases i` lemmas **also** times out, because heartbeats are budgeted
per *declaration*: 24 cases of ~2.5 s each blow a single 200 000-heartbeat
budget. One declaration per fact is not verbosity, it is what keeps each one
inside its own budget.

## Provenance of the tables

Generated by inverting `mulσᵢ`'s own definition — for each `i`, the entries in
which `v i` occurs — and shape-checked: every `i` occurs in **exactly one** entry
with coefficient `1` and in **at most one** with coefficient `h`. That check is
the "at most two" claim, verified rather than assumed. The statements below are
then re-checked by Lean against `mulσᵢ` itself, so a transcription error cannot
survive.
-/
import QOU.Machinery.HeckeH4AlgebraLaws

namespace QOU.Machinery.H4

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R]

/-! ## `trM4` on a basis vector, over a `CommRing`

`QOU.Hecke.MarkovTraceOnH4A.trM4_e₄` is this over a `Field`. The proof uses no
inverses and everything below is stated over `R`. -/

theorem trM4_e₄' (z h : R) (j : Fin 24) : trM4 z h (e₄ (R := R) j) = TRM4 z h j := by
  simp [trM4, e₄, Pi.single_apply, Finset.sum_ite_eq']

